[{:db/id -1, :adr/id "adr-2606031600-kotoba-os-content-addressed-wasm-unikernel", :adr/title "ADR-2606031600: kotoba-os — a content-addressed, WASM-first Rust unikernel OS for OT / PLC / k8s, agent-centric (Holochain) over the kotoba Datom log (Datomic preserved)", :adr/status "proposed", :adr/doc_type "adr", :adr/source-format "edn", :adr/body "\n# ADR-2606031600: kotoba-os — content-addressed WASM-first Rust unikernel OS\n\n**Status**: proposed\n**Date**: 2026-06-03\n**Deciders**: Jun Kawasaki (author), Council Lv6+ ≥3 (ratify per phase; OT/PLC live-actuation + hard-RT/safety phases require additional Lv7+ gate per N1/N3)\n**ADR hierarchy**: System-software extension of the substrate-engine charter. ADR-2605262130 named **kotoba** the canonical storage substrate (17 crates) and ADR-2605312345 made the **Datom log** the first-class canonical state. This ADR takes that same substrate and gives it a **lower edge** — it packages kotoba as an *operating system*: a single-address-space Rust unikernel (the way **Hermit** packages a Rust app as a bootable library OS) whose userland is **content-addressed WASM Component Model actors** and whose canonical machine state is the **same kotoba Datom log**. Nothing about the Datom model, the encrypted wire format, the Murakumo-only inference invariant, the no-server-key rule, or the land/SBT/Council on-chain carve-outs changes. What is new is *where kotoba runs and what it boots into*.\n\n> **Closing status (2026-06-03)**: R0 charter + a fully tested monorepo-side reference landed via PR #884 + #891 (+ a fieldbus follow-up): validated `kotoba:os` WIT contract, genesis + OCI-CID schemas, the `kotoba-os-types` Rust crate (manifest validate/authorize + `LowerEdge` real CIDv1-blake3 verify + mesh source-chain/quorum/membrane), **three real WASM components** (plc-control / mesh-agent / modbus-control), and a wasmtime host runner (scan-cycle=Datom-transaction + N3 atomicity + fuel-bounded soft-RT + multi-actor-one-log). **66 tests** via `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/reference/run-all.sh`. Honest boundaries hold: no unikernel boot on hardware, no live device I/O, production crate pending in the `40-engine/kotoba` subrepo. **ADR-id note**: `2606031600` collides with the ipaddress/yabai ADR (parallel-agent-race; cf. 2605263400/2605263500) — disambiguated by filename + topic, renumber deferred to a future ADR-id reconciliation. Session-close: ADR-2606036000. **R2 broadening (session-close ADR-2606037000)**: a 4th WASM component proving all 8 device interfaces bind; a wasm32 browser-edge (L1c) build gate; `LowerEdge::boot_actor` capability authorization in the boot path; mesh source-chain monotone-growth e2e. **70 tests**. **Loop end (session-close ADR-2606038000)**: + `PORTING.md` reference->production handoff (each artifact -> its kotoba crate, the `datom.fact`->`kqe.quad` field mapping, the tests as acceptance spec); ~75 tests. The monorepo-side reference is complete; remaining work is upstream in the `40-engine/kotoba` subrepo (N6). **Unikernel boot PoC (session-close ADR-2606039500)**: `reference/boot-poc/` — a no_std aarch64 image BOOTS on QEMU virt, does real PL011 MMIO I/O, runs the scan/N3 model in-kernel, runs a real core-wasm module under wasmi in-kernel, and reads strings from the wasm guest's linear memory (live-verified, PRs #995/#1000/#1003/#1005). The full Component Model ABI + Hermit kernel remain the production crate.\n\n## Context\n\n### The request\n\n> *kotoba を unikernel をベースにした OT, PLC, k8s などでも使える OS としての設計に拡張。WASM を前提に、Hermit・Holochain を参考に、IPFS ベース、Datomic の発想は変えずに、content-addressed Rust unikernel OS。*\n\nToday kotoba is a **library / server / browser-wasm** substrate: you `brew install kotoba`, run `kotoba serve`, and apps reach it through `@etzhayyim/sdk`. It is excellent as a *database engine*. The request is to push it one layer down — to make kotoba the **system software** of a node, so that a field PLC, a factory-floor controller (OT), and a k8s pod are all *the same content-addressed WASM substrate*, differing only in their bottom edge (bare metal vs hypervisor vs container vs browser).\n\n### Why a unikernel, and why WASM as the premise\n\nThree properties the religious-corp substrate already wants, that a WASM-first unikernel delivers natively:\n\n1. **Content-addressability all the way down.** kotoba already makes *state* a CID (the Datom log). A unikernel makes the *machine image itself* a single, statically-linked artifact — which can also be a CID. WASM components are already content-addressed (ADR-2606014500/14600: actors = WASM on IPFS, CID-verified, run browser-local or on the donated mesh). So the whole stack — kernel image, userland component, configuration, and state — collapses into one content-addressed graph. **\"Boot a CID, reconstruct its Datom state, join the neighbourhood.\"**\n\n2. **A single, auditable, replayable execution domain.** A unikernel is one address space, one application, no general-purpose multi-user surface. That is exactly the right shape for an OT/PLC field device (minimal attack surface, deterministic boot, no shell) *and* for a k8s pod (one workload per microVM). Combined with the Datom log, every machine becomes a *time-travelling, replayable* control system: every scan cycle's inputs and outputs are immutable Datoms with `as-of` history (non-eschatological — no final state, ADR-2605192100 §1.15 echoed at the machine layer).\n\n3. **WASM as the portable, sandboxed, deterministic userland.** The premise (`WASM を前提に`) is that the *unit of execution everywhere* is a WASM Component Model component with WIT-typed imports. The control program for a PLC, the agent for a mesh node, and the Pregel cell for a religious-corp actor are the **same artifact kind** — a content-addressed `.wasm` component — sandboxed by the runtime, portable across the three lower edges, and validated against content-addressed rules before it runs. kotoba already ships `kotoba-runtime` (wasmtime Component Model host) + `kotoba-guest` (WIT bindings) + `kotoba-vm` (WasmPregel); this ADR makes them the OS userland, not just a UDF sandbox.\n\n### Prior art we build on (and what we take from each)\n\n- **Hermit / RustyHermit (Hermit OS)** — a Rust-written, `no_std`, single-address-space **library operating system / unikernel**. An application is *linked into* the kernel and boots directly on KVM / Firecracker / QEMU / bare metal (and on Hermit's own lightweight `uhyve` hypervisor), targeting x86-64 and aarch64. We take its **packaging model** (app + minimal kernel = one bootable image), its **Rust `no_std` core**, and its **microVM-friendly boot path**. We *invert its userland*: instead of linking one native Rust app, kotoba-os links a **WASM runtime + the kotoba substrate crates**, and the \"application\" is a content-addressed WASM component loaded at boot. (This is the same direction as `wasmtime`-on-Hermit and the broader \"unikernel-as-WASM-host\" line of work.)\n\n- **Holochain** — **agent-centric** (not data-centric) distributed computing: every agent keeps a local, append-only, hash-linked **source chain** of its own actions; a validating, sharded, content-addressed **DHT** stores published entries; **membrane / validation rules** (the DNA) decide what is valid; random neighbour validators witness. kotoba *already* carries the Holochain-isomorphic primitives — `kotoba-dht` ships **Source Chain / Warrant / Neighborhood / Availability Proof** (it is the engine that superseded the explicitly-Holochain-iso kotoba-datomic spec, ADR-2605231400). This ADR realizes that model **as an OS**: each kotoba-os node *is* a Holochain-style agent, **its source chain IS its local Datom-log segment**, its **DNA is the content-addressed WASM component + validation rules**, and its neighbours are the Murakumo/donated mesh (ADR-2605214000 + ADR-2606012100).\n\n- **IPFS** — the **block backend** for everything content-addressed (CIDv1 blake3 per `kotoba-core`): the kernel image, the WASM components, the genesis config, and the cold tier of Datom blocks. This is unchanged from ADR-2605312345 (IPFS = block backend / DHT, not canonical state home).\n\n- **Datomic** — **unchanged and central.** The Datom 5-tuple `(E, A, V, T, Added)`, immutable, content-addressed, indexed by the kotoba-kqe arrangements (EAVT / AEVT / AVET / VAET / TEA), remains the canonical state — now extended to describe the *machine itself* (process table, device registry, peer set, control-loop I/O), not only application data. **`Datomic の発想は変えずに`** is honoured as a hard constraint: kotoba-os introduces **no** mutable, in-place, bypass-the-log state store on the node (N7).\n\n### Where this lands relative to existing node classes\n\nADR-2606012100 already recognises three donated-compute node forms (**ameno** browser WebGPU, **e7m** CLI node, **kotoba** IPFS+Datom pod). ADR-2606014500/14600 already run **content-addressed WASM actors** (one CF Worker, many WASM actors; gateway + loader + componentize-py; tsumugi/watatsuna/kanae PoCs). kotoba-os **unifies and generalizes** these: ameno = kotoba-os on the *browser edge*, e7m = kotoba-os on the *hosted-process edge*, the field PLC / k8s pod = kotoba-os on the *unikernel edge*. One OS, three lower edges, the same content-addressed WASM userland and the same Datom-log state.\n\n## Decision\n\nreligious-corp adopts **kotoba-os**: a content-addressed, WASM-first system-software shape for the kotoba substrate. kotoba-os is **not a new engine and not a fork** (N6) — it is a **new top crate + boot/runtime configuration** of the existing `40-engine/kotoba/` workspace plus a thin Hermit-derived kernel layer, governed by the substrate's existing constitutional carve-outs.\n\n### The five-layer model\n\n```\n┌──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│ L5  Actors            content-addressed WASM Component Model    │  ← .wasm from IPFS, CID-verified\n│                       components: PLC control program /         │     before load (ADR-2606014600)\n│                       mesh agent / religious-corp Pregel cell   │\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ L4  kotoba runtime    WASM host + WIT device/world interfaces   │  ← kotoba-runtime, kotoba-guest,\n│                       (fuel-metered, capability-scoped)         │     kotoba-vm (WasmPregel)\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ L3  kotoba substrate  STATE = Datom log (canonical)             │  ← kotoba-core/kqe/kse/graph/\n│                       TRANSPORT = libp2p; DHT = Holochain-iso   │     store / net / dht / crypto / signal\n│                       source-chain/warrant/neighbourhood        │     (ADR-2605312345 unchanged)\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ L2  kotoba-os kernel  Hermit-derived no_std unikernel:          │  ← NEW thin layer: schedule / mem /\n│                       scheduler · memory · net stack · device   │     net / device-WIT shim; single\n│                       bus shim (modbus/opcua/ethercat/GPIO)     │     address space, no shell\n├──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤\n│ L1  lower edge        (a) bare-metal / microVM unikernel        │  ← KVM/Firecracker/uhyve · PLC SoC\n│                       (b) hosted Linux process (e7m / sidecar)  │     · containerd-runwasi · browser\n│                       (c) browser wasm32 (ameno)                │     wasm32 (kotoba-store-web)\n└──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nThe crucial property: **L3–L5 are identical across all three L1 edges.** Only L1/L2 swap. A control program validated and tested in the browser edge runs bit-for-bit (modulo device I/O availability) on the unikernel edge, because it is the same content-addressed WASM component over the same Datom substrate.\n\n### D1. The boot model — boot a CID, reconstruct the Datom, join the neighbourhood\n\nA kotoba-os node boots from a **genesis CID** (its \"DNA\", in Holochain terms): a content-addressed manifest naming (i) the kernel image CID, (ii) the userland WASM component CID(s) + their validation-rule CIDs, (iii) the device/world WIT bindings it is permitted to import (capability set), and (iv) the neighbourhood / mesh join parameters. **The manifest contract is authored and tested**: JSON Schema (draft 2020-12) at `00-contracts/schemas/kotoba-os-genesis-manifest.schema.json` with a valid example + a negative fixture + 3 passing tests; it encodes the constitutional carve-outs structurally — `identity.serverKey: const false` (C3/N5), `safety.liveActuation: const false` (N3), `safety.civilianOnly: const true` (N1), and `capabilities.interfaces` enumerated from the `kotoba:os` WIT package (capability scoping). Boot sequence:\n\n1. Verify the kernel image against its CID (the image *is* a CID; a tampered image fails verification — same trustless `/ipfs/<cid>` re-verify discipline as ADR-2606014600). **Real verification landed** (R2): `kotoba-os-types::cid` computes CIDv1(raw, blake3) matching kotoba-core, and `LowerEdge::verify_artifact(bytes, cid)` recomputes-and-compares (rejects tampered bytes) — its from-scratch base32 encoder is validated against an independent oracle; 6 cargo tests.\n2. Reconstruct local Datom state: from on-device cold blocks if present, else from IPFS + the Base L2 anchor (the disaster-recovery property preserved in ADR-2605312345 — the Datom log is deterministically reconstructible from MST + IPFS + L2).\n3. CID-verify each userland WASM component **before** load (ADR-2606014500/14600 discipline), check it against its content-addressed validation rules (the membrane). The boot path also enforces **capability authorization** — `LowerEdge::boot_actor` refuses an actor whose WASM imports the manifest does not grant (validate carve-outs + authorize imports + CID-verify; `kotoba-os-types`, 21 cargo tests).\n4. Join the DHT neighbourhood (`kotoba-net` libp2p QUIC/Noise/GossipSub + `kotoba-dht` Source Chain / Warrant / Neighborhood); the node's local Datom-log segment is its **source chain**.\n5. Start L5 actors; begin transacting.\n\n**Reproducibility**: same genesis CID → same boot → same initial machine. This is the unikernel/content-addressing payoff and it directly serves the multi-generational reproducibility surface (Charter Rider §2(f)).\n\n### D2. The userland is WASM Component Model — one artifact kind for control / agent / cell\n\nEvery L5 unit is a WASM Component Model component with **WIT-typed imports** and **no ambient authority** — it can touch only the device worlds and substrate capabilities its genesis manifest granted (capability-scoped, fuel-metered via the runtime). The same artifact kind serves:\n\n- a **PLC control program** (imports `kotoba:io/*` device worlds; see D3),\n- a **mesh agent** (imports `kotoba:substrate/datom` + `kotoba:net/gossip`),\n- a **religious-corp Pregel cell** (imports `kotoba:vm/pregel`; the kotodama/kami-engine cell catalog of ADR-2605192415 becomes loadable here, complementary to LangGraph cells, exactly as `kotoba-vm` is \"complementary, not a replacement\" per ADR-2605262130 row 14).\n\nThis unifies ADR-2606014500's \"one Worker, many WASM actors\" with the field/k8s edges: the *actor* is portable; only the *host edge* differs. **Demonstrated end-to-end**: `plc-host-runner` instantiates BOTH the plc-control and mesh-agent components into ONE wasmtime store (one HostState = one Datom log) and interleaves control scans + agent steps — the shared log holds the control commands AND the agent heartbeats (2 each). One OS node, many content-addressed WASM actors, one Datom log. **First real L5 artifact landed**: `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/reference/plc-control-guest/` compiles a bang-bang controller to an actual WASM Component-Model component implementing the `plc-control` world (wit-bindgen → wasm32 → `wasm-tools component new`; validates under `--features component-model`; 22.8 KB). Its imports are **capability-minimized by construction** — wit-bindgen tree-shakes the world to exactly `io-analog`/`io-digital`/`datom` (the interfaces the controller calls); `fieldbus-*`/`io-gpio` do not appear in the component's world. 3 build-and-verify tests green (toolchain-guarded). Built with plain cargo + wit-bindgen rather than cargo-component (blocked in-env by a malformed global wasm-pkg config), so no fork/dependency on broken tooling.\n\n### D3. OT / PLC — the scan cycle is a Datom transaction (the novel control model)\n\nThe industrial-control angle is the sharpest expression of \"Datomic unchanged, applied to a new domain.\" A kotoba-os PLC node runs a classic **scan cycle** — read inputs → execute control logic → write outputs → housekeeping — where:\n\n- **Inputs/outputs are WIT-typed device worlds.** We define a small family of content-addressed WIT interfaces: `kotoba:io/digital` (discrete I/O), `kotoba:io/analog`, and fieldbus worlds `kotoba:fieldbus/{modbus, opcua, ethercat, canopen}` plus `kotoba:io/gpio` for SoC field devices. The contract is authored + machine-validated at `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/kotoba-os.wit` (`wasm-tools component wit` EXIT 0). The L2 kernel implements the device-bus shim; the L5 control program imports only what its capability set allows. IEC 61131-3 control logic (ladder / structured text) compiles to a WASM component that imports these worlds — **WASM-as-PLC**.\n- **Every scan cycle writes Datoms.** Each cycle's input snapshot, the logic's decisions, and the output commands are recorded as an immutable Datom transaction with `T` = cycle index. The result is a **fully auditable, replayable control history**: you can reconstruct the exact state of the machine at any cycle (`as-of`), replay a fault, and prove (on-chain-anchored, via the L2 trust anchor) what the controller commanded and when. For industrial safety forensics and accountability this is a genuine differentiator — most PLCs have shallow, mutable, overwrite-in-place memory; here the control trace is content-addressed and immutable. The semantics are pinned by an executable reference model + 6 tests at `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/reference/`, **and verified end-to-end through actual WASM**: `reference/plc-host-runner/` instantiates the real `plc-control` component under wasmtime, drives scan cycles, and confirms both the scan-cycle Datom history (`as-of` reconstructs ON/OFF/ON) and N3 fault-atomicity (a faulted sensor read makes the guest return `Err` and commits zero datoms) — 3 e2e tests green.\n- **Scope (civilian only).** OT/PLC control under this ADR is for the religious-corp **civilian producing actors** — tatekata/giemon construction robotics (ADR-2605250715, ADR-2606010030), hikari energy grid-edge (ADR-2605261100), mizuho water/sanitation, mitsuho agriculture, the factory lines (giemon/sarutahiko/funadaiku). Weapons / fire-control / kinetic military actuation is a **hard constitutional prohibition** (N3 + Charter Rider §2(a) + force-separation, ADR-2605192100 §1.12).\n- **Honest real-time framing.** R0 targets **soft-RT / scan-cycle determinism** (bounded, jitter-reduced cycles in a single-address-space unikernel with pinned cores, AOT-compiled WASM via Cranelift or deterministic `wasmi`, GC-free linear memory, pre-allocated buffers, fuel metering for WCET estimation). **Fuel metering demonstrated** (`plc-host-runner`): wasmtime `consume_fuel` measures per-scan fuel (~1.4-1.8k units/scan -- a WCET-estimation input) and proves **bounded execution** -- a budget below the per-scan cost traps the guest, so a misbehaving control program cannot run unbounded (the enforceable soft-RT primitive). **Hard-RT and functional-safety certification (IEC 61508 SIL2/3/4, IEC 61131-3 conformance, safety-instrumented systems) are explicitly OUT of R0 scope and Lv7+ gated** (N2). kotoba-os does not replace a certified safety PLC for a safety function at R0; it is the *control + audit* substrate alongside (not inside) the safety loop until certified.\n\n### D4. k8s — kotoba-os as a content-addressed microVM / runwasi node class\n\nA kotoba-os unikernel image is **OCI-packageable** and runnable under k8s two ways, both already aligned with existing infra:\n\n1. **microVM** (Firecracker / Kata / Hermit `uhyve`) wrapped as an OCI image — each pod is one unikernel, scheduled by the existing **Murakumo kubelet** (`50-infra/k8s/murakumo-kubelet`, ADR-2605214000 no-VKE mesh).\n2. **containerd-runwasi shim** — for the hosted-process / wasm32 edge, the WASM component runs directly under a WASM containerd shim, no microVM.\n\nThe **image reference is a CID**, pulled from IPFS, not from a commercial container registry — a content-addressed OCI artifact whose digest *is* the kotoba CID. This keeps the k8s edge inside the blockchain-self-contained / IPFS-based substrate (no Docker Hub / GHCR dependency for first-party images) and inside the donation-funded mesh model (ADR-2606012100): donating a kotoba-os node to the mesh is donating compute, in the same `donated` node class. **Contract + invariant tested**: `00-contracts/schemas/kotoba-os-oci-artifact.schema.json` (+ example + 8 tests) makes \"digest = CID\" a *checkable* equivalence — the OCI manifest sha2-256 digest and the `cid` are the same hash re-encoded (`cid = base32(0x01 0x55 0x12 0x20 || digest)`), and the test decodes the CID back to a digest and asserts equality. The schema forbids commercial registries by construction (`imageRef` must be `ipfs://bafkre…`; a `ghcr.io` ref is rejected), pull is IPFS-only, placement is the Murakumo kubelet.\n\n### D5. Agent-centric mesh — source chain = local Datom segment; membrane = content-addressed validation\n\nFollowing Holochain, each kotoba-os node is an **agent** with a local append-only **source chain** — which is **exactly its local Datom-log segment** (no new structure; the source chain *is* the per-node EAVT log, `kotoba-dht::SourceChain` over the `kotoba-kqe` arrangements). Published entries propagate to the validating, sharded DHT (`kotoba-dht` Neighborhood + Availability Proof). The **membrane / validation rules** are content-addressed WASM (the DNA): a node accepts a peer's Datoms only if they validate against the rule component named in the shared genesis. Witness quorum is the existing deterministic rule (witness index = `hash(record_cid) + i mod len(witnesses)`, ADR-2605231902, unchanged). This is the same witness/warrant machinery kotoba already ships, now framed as the OS's consensus membrane. **Reference-modelled + tested**: `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/reference/kotoba-os-types/src/mesh.rs` implements the source chain (append-only, hash-linked, tamper-detecting `verify()`), the deterministic witness selection, and a `Membrane` + `quorum_accepts()` — 7 cargo tests green (incl. chain tamper-detection, witness determinism/reproducibility, capability-membrane rejection, quorum acceptance).\n\n### D6. Three lower edges, one substrate — the baien edge-target invariant applies\n\nThe browser edge (L1c) is bound by the **baien edge-target invariant** (ADR-2605241900): kotoba-os-in-browser must run within the WASM-32 + iPhone 12+ + Android 4GB envelope, using `kotoba-store-web` (IndexedDB block store) for the content-addressed cache and respecting the ≤200 MB heap carve-out from ADR-2605262130 Phase 4. The unikernel edge (L1a) for a field PLC targets small SoCs (the kernel is `no_std`; the WASM runtime + minimal substrate must fit constrained flash/RAM). **Minimal boot demonstrated** (`reference/boot-poc/`): a `no_std` single-address-space aarch64 image boots on `qemu-system-aarch64 -machine virt` (the Firecracker/KVM machine class), enters at `_start`, and writes to the **real PL011 UART via MMIO** (`0x09000000`) — a genuine if minimal unikernel-boot + memory-mapped device-I/O PoC, and it brings up a bump-allocator heap and **runs the kotoba-os scan-cycle=Datom-transaction model (incl. N3 fault-atomicity) inside the booted unikernel**, printing the Datom log over the real UART (1 toolchain-guarded test). It also runs a **real core-wasm module under the wasmi interpreter inside the booted unikernel** (the module imports kernel-provided host functions and produces Datoms, and the host reads command **strings from the guest's linear memory** — the primitive real components use for Fact strings; `_start` enables FP/SIMD at EL1 which wasmi needs). What it does NOT yet do is host the full WASM **Component Model** via `kotoba-runtime` in-unikernel — that (and Hermit's full kernel) is the production crate, `PORTING.md`. **First-cut sizing budget landed** at `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/sizing-budget.json` (+ 7 consistency tests): three device tiers (T0 MCU honestly excluded as a non-target · T1 constrained SoC 64–256 MiB = minimal target · T2 edge gateway), per-component RAM/flash estimate ranges, and a minimal-resident profile (wasmi runtime + minimal substrate + 1 actor) whose low-end ~14 MiB RAM fits the 64 MiB T1 floor. **HONEST**: every number is an engineering ESTIMATE, not a measurement; real footprints are an R2 deliverable (build the Hermit + runtime + substrate image and measure), and the tests verify the budget's internal consistency, not the truth of the estimates. The hosted edge (L1b) is the e7m node of ADR-2606012100, unchanged. **Browser-edge viability proven** (L1c): the `kotoba-os-types` substrate crate (genesis manifest + LowerEdge + CIDv1-blake3 verify + mesh) compiles cleanly to `wasm32-unknown-unknown` — the baien WASM-32 target — as a permanent `run-all.sh` gate; the full ≤200 MB heap / iPhone-12 / Android-4GB envelope test remains R4.\n\n### Constitutional carve-outs preserved (each restated; none weakened)\n\n- **C1 — Datom canonical, unchanged.** kotoba-os adds an OS *shape*; the Datom log remains the first-class canonical state (ADR-2605312345). The machine's own management-plane state (process/actor table, device registry, peer set) is *additionally* modelled as Datoms (the \"Datomic-for-the-OS\" property), but no application semantics change.\n- **C2 — Murakumo-only inference.** kotoba-os nodes run **no local LLM inference**. `kotoba-llm` stays a routing facade to the Murakumo gateway (LiteLLM 127.0.0.1:4000 / EVO-X2 LAN), local-inference features disabled in religious-corp builds (ADR-2605215000 + ADR-2605262130 D2). A PLC/OT node does control, not inference.\n- **C3 — No server-key.** A kotoba-os node holds **no platform-held private key**. Device/node identity is a **member/operator-delegated capability** (CACAO chain + delegation via `kotoba-auth`, the same allowed-capability set as ADR-2605231525): member-wallet, member-passkey-ES256, community-operator DID for bulk-ingest, Council 5-of-7 Safe for governance. Read-only kotoba-os surfaces carry the `// no-server-key: read-only` marker; enforced by `e7m verify` 9th invariant.\n- **C4 — On-chain records stay on-chain.** Land / SBT / Council attestation / Public Fund / Tithe / Force Authorization records are NOT written by a kotoba-os node as primary home (geth-private + Base L2 + IPFS + LANDS.md/MEMBERS.md remain authoritative; ADR-2605262130 D4/N3). A node may index/mirror them locally.\n- **C5 — License.** kotoba-os crates inherit Apache 2.0 + Charter Compliance Rider v2.0 (ADR-2605192200), first-party. The Hermit-derived kernel layer: Hermit OS is MIT/Apache-2.0 dual-licensed, so vendoring is license-compatible; vendored 3rd-party kernel code keeps its original NOTICE (no Rider on `vendor/`/`*-fork/` per repo Do-Not rules).\n- **C6 — Substrate boundary.** Apps reach kotoba-os only via `@etzhayyim/sdk`; no direct `kotoba-*` import from `60-apps/*` (ADR-2605172000 + ADR-2605262130 D5).\n- **C7 — Encrypted wire format unchanged.** Datoms gossiped between nodes carry `com.etzhayyim.encrypted.*` ciphertext for private records (ADR-2605181100/181200), implemented by `kotoba-crypto` + `kotoba-signal`; never plaintext private records on the wire.\n\n## Consequences\n\n- **New first-party crate(s)**: `kotoba-os` (boot + L2 kernel shim + device-WIT host) plus a small set of WIT world definitions (`kotoba:io/*`, `kotoba:fieldbus/*`) authored in `00-contracts/` and the kotoba workspace. These are **additive** to the 17 existing kotoba crates; the kernel layer vendors Hermit (`vendor/hermit/` or submodule, 3rd-party NOTICE preserved).\n- **Three build targets** from one source: `wasm32` (browser/runwasi), native hosted (`x86_64`/`aarch64` Linux process), and unikernel (`x86_64`/`aarch64` microVM/bare-metal). CI matrix is an R1 deliverable.\n- **k8s**: a content-addressed OCI artifact convention (digest = CID, pulled from IPFS) lands as an R2 deliverable; Murakumo kubelet gains a kotoba-os pod class. No change to existing pods.\n- **OT/PLC actors** (tatekata/giemon/hikari/mizuho/mitsuho/factory lines) gain a *real control + audit substrate* path: their simulated control loops (today kami-engine physics sims) can target the kotoba-os WASM-PLC interface, with scan-cycle Datom history. **Live actuation on physical hardware remains Council+operator gated** (the existing G11/outward-gated pattern across every actor ADR).\n- **No code change is required to ratify R0** — like ADR-2605262130 Phase 0, R0 is the charter + WIT-world drafts + the `kotoba-os` crate scaffold (boot manifest type + edge-abstraction trait), no runtime cutover. The phased rollout is the implementation vehicle.\n- **CLAUDE.md / deps.toml**: a Status row + a `[[modules]]` entry register kotoba-os; the substrate-boundary table gains kotoba-os as a runtime shape (not a new state layer — State row unchanged).\n\n### Phased rollout (R0 → R5)\n\n- **R0 — Charter (this ADR).** `kotoba-os` crate scaffold: genesis-manifest type, the `LowerEdge` trait abstracting L1 (bare-metal / hosted / browser), and the `kotoba:io/*` + `kotoba:fieldbus/*` WIT world drafts. No boot, no device I/O. Exit: Council Lv6+ ≥3 on this text. **Landed**: the device + control WIT contract is authored and machine-validated at `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/kotoba-os.wit` (`wasm-tools component wit` EXIT 0; interfaces `io-{digital,analog,gpio}` + `fieldbus-{modbus,opcua,ethercat,canopen}` + `datom`; worlds `plc-control` [scan cycle = Datom transaction] + `mesh-agent`; N1/N2/N3/N7 encoded — stage-only writes, soft-RT `duration-us`, no live actuation) and the genesis-manifest boot contract is authored + tested at `00-contracts/schemas/kotoba-os-genesis-manifest.schema.json`. Crate scaffold landed too: `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/reference/kotoba-os-types/` — the genesis manifest as typed Rust (serde `deny_unknown_fields`), `GenesisManifest::validate()` enforcing C3/N5+N3+N1, capability scoping, and the `LowerEdge` trait with an edge-independent `boot()` + `HostedEdge` impl (6 `cargo test` green). The production crate (with real blake3 CID verification + wasmtime host) lands in the kotoba subrepo via upstream coordination (N6).\n- **R1 — Hosted edge + WASM userland.** kotoba-os runs as a hosted Linux process (the e7m edge) hosting one CID-verified WASM Component Model actor over the live Datom log; sizing budget for the unikernel edge published (honest flash/RAM numbers). Exit: a sample agent component boots from a genesis CID, transacts Datoms, joins a 3-node neighbourhood. **Partial landed (pre-host)**: an executable, stdlib-only reference model of the scan-cycle semantics at `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/reference/` pins the D3/N2/N3/N7 invariants with 6 green tests (staged-not-applied-mid-cycle, faulted-cycle-commits-nothing, one-txn-per-cycle-T=cycle, Datomic `as-of` reconstruction, bus-state-is-a-pure-log-projection, append-only-immutable-log) plus the genesis-manifest schema with 3 green tests — the semantics the real Rust host must honor, regression-tested before the host exists.\n- **R2 — Unikernel edge + k8s.** Hermit-derived `no_std` boot on Firecracker/`uhyve`; OCI-CID artifact convention; Murakumo kubelet kotoba-os pod class; one religious-corp cell runs in a unikernel pod. Exit: reproducible boot (same CID → same machine) + e7m verify clean.\n- **R3 — OT/PLC soft-RT.** `kotoba:io/digital`+`analog`+`modbus`+`opcua` device-bus shim on a constrained SoC; a WASM control program runs a soft-RT scan loop with per-cycle Datom history; **simulation/bench only, no live actuation.** Exit: scan-cycle determinism characterised (jitter bounds honestly reported), replay-from-Datom verified, one actor (e.g., hikari grid-edge or tatekata) drives it in sim.\n- **R4 — browser edge parity.** kotoba-os-in-ameno (wasm32) runs the *same* control/agent component within the baien edge envelope (≤200 MB heap, iPhone 12+ / Android 4GB). Exit: bit-identical actor behaviour across hosted/unikernel/browser edges on a test corpus.\n- **R5 (deferred, Lv7+ gated) — hard-RT / safety / live actuation.** Functional-safety path (IEC 61508 SILx), certified-PLC interop, and physical actuation on civilian-actor hardware. **Each requires Council Lv7+ + operator gate + the force-separation review (no weapons/fire-control, ever).**\n\n## Non-goals (N1..N8, IMMUTABLE R0..R5)\n\n- **N1 — Not a weapons / military / fire-control RTOS.** kotoba-os OT/PLC control is for civilian producing actors only. No kinetic military actuation, no weapons control loop, ever. Constitutional invariant (Charter Rider §2(a) + ADR-2605192100 §1.12 force-separation). Lv7+ cannot amend §2(a).\n- **N2 — R0..R4 are NOT a certified safety system.** No IEC 61508 SIL / IEC 61131-3 conformance / safety-instrumented-system claim until R5, which is Lv7+ gated. kotoba-os does not sit inside a safety function at R0; honesty about hard-RT limits of WASM is mandatory in every artifact.\n- **N3 — No live physical actuation without operator + Council gate.** Same outward-gated (G11) discipline as every actor ADR. Sim/bench is R0..R3; live hardware is R5.\n- **N4 — No local LLM inference on kotoba-os nodes.** Murakumo-only (ADR-2605215000); `kotoba-llm` facade only.\n- **N5 — No server-held private key on any kotoba-os node.** Delegated capability only (ADR-2605231525).\n- **N6 — No fork of kotoba, Hermit, or wasmtime.** Subrepo/submodule + upstream-PR; vendored 3rd-party keeps its NOTICE (no Rider on `vendor/`). Escalation via ADR amendment, not fork.\n- **N7 — Datomic philosophy unchanged.** No mutable, in-place, bypass-the-log state store on the node. All durable state is Datoms; the machine's management plane is *additionally* Datom-modelled, never a side mutable store. (`Datomic の発想は変えずに`.)\n- **N8 — Not a general-purpose multi-user desktop/server OS.** Single-address-space, single-workload unikernel by design (that is its security and determinism story). No shell, no general POSIX multiuser surface on the unikernel edge.\n\n## Alternatives Considered\n\n**A. Stay a library/server only; run on a host Linux + Docker.** Status quo. Rejected for the OT/PLC and content-addressed-image goals: a full Linux + Docker stack has a large mutable surface, no content-addressed boot image, and no single-address-space determinism story for field control. The hosted edge (L1b) is *retained* for dev/sidecar, but it is one of three edges, not the only shape.\n\n**B. Build a kotoba RTOS from scratch.** Greenfield `no_std` kernel. Rejected: Hermit already provides a Rust `no_std`, microVM-bootable, x86-64/aarch64 unikernel under MIT/Apache-2.0 (license-compatible), and the broader ecosystem already does wasmtime-on-unikernel. Re-implementing the kernel is unbounded effort with no constitutional benefit (same reasoning as ADR-2605262130 Alt-B greenfield rejection).\n\n**C. Native (non-WASM) userland linked into the unikernel (pure Hermit model).** Rejected against the explicit premise (`WASM を前提に`). A native-linked userland loses content-addressed portability, the cross-edge \"same artifact\" property, the capability-scoped sandbox, and the alignment with the existing one-Worker-many-WASM-actors design (ADR-2606014500/14600). WASM Component Model is the unit *because* it is content-addressed, portable across all three edges, and sandboxable for OT.\n\n**D. Adopt an existing WASM edge OS / framework (e.g., a generic Wasm-on-unikernel stack) wholesale instead of extending kotoba.** Rejected: the value is *the Datom substrate as the machine's state and the agent-centric DHT as the mesh* — neither is provided by a generic WASM unikernel. kotoba-os is precisely \"a WASM unikernel **whose state is the kotoba Datom log and whose mesh is the kotoba/Holochain-iso DHT**.\" Drop those and it is no longer the religious-corp substrate.\n\n**E. Make the unikernel the canonical state home (state lives in the kernel image).** Rejected: violates ADR-2605312345 — the Datom log is canonical; the kernel image and node are *materializations/agents over* the Datom log, deterministically reconstructible from IPFS + L2. The machine is disposable; the Datom is not.\n\n## References\n\n- ADR-2605262130 (Kotoba as Canonical Storage Substrate) — parent; the 17-crate engine kotoba-os packages as an OS\n- ADR-2605312345 (kotoba Datom as First-Class Canonical State) — Datom canonical; D1/C1/Alt-E reconstruction guarantee\n- ADR-2605231400 (kotoba-datomic Holochain-iso substrate) — Holochain-isomorphic source-chain/DHT lineage (superseded by 2605262130; conceptual reference for D5)\n- ADR-2605214000 (Murakumo mesh, no-VKE) — the mesh kotoba-os nodes join; kubelet\n- ADR-2606012100 (Donation-funded operation + compute-node donation) — ameno/e7m/kotoba node classes kotoba-os unifies\n- ADR-2606014500 (One Worker, many WASM actors) — content-addressed WASM actor model (L5)\n- ADR-2606014600 (WASM-actor runtime: gateway + loader + componentize-py) — CID re-verify discipline at boot/load\n- ADR-2605215000 (inference Murakumo-only) — C2/N4\n- ADR-2605231525 (no-server-key religious-corp architecture) — C3/N5\n- ADR-2605181100 / 181200 (encrypted records + Signal key-wrap) — C7\n- ADR-2605241900 (baien edge-target invariant) — D6/R4 browser-edge envelope\n- ADR-2605192200 (Apache 2.0 + Charter Compliance Rider v2.0) — C5 license\n- ADR-2605192100 (Mission Charter) — §1.12 force-separation, §1.15 non-eschatological (no final machine state), N1\n- ADR-2605192415 (Religious-Corp Daemon Architecture) — Pregel cell catalog loadable as L5 actors\n- ADR-2605250715 (tatekata) / ADR-2605261100 (hikari) / ADR-2606010030 (giemon/factory robotics) — civilian OT/PLC consumer actors (D3)\n- Hermit OS (RustyHermit) — Rust `no_std` single-address-space unikernel / library OS (MIT/Apache-2.0); microVM boot model\n- Holochain — agent-centric source-chain + validating DHT + membrane/DNA model\n- `40-engine/kotoba/README.edn` — KOTOBA equation; `kotoba-runtime` / `kotoba-guest` / `kotoba-vm` WASM Component Model surface\n- `00-contracts/wit/kotoba-os/` — the `kotoba:os` WIT device + control contract + executable reference model + tests\n- `00-contracts/schemas/kotoba-os-genesis-manifest.schema.json` — the D1 genesis-manifest boot contract\n", :adr/path "90-docs/adr/2606031600-kotoba-os-content-addressed-wasm-unikernel.edn", :adr/source-repo "root", :adr/topic "storage-substrate", :adr/last_verified "2026-06-03", :adr/date "2026-06-03", :adr/authoritative true, :adr/related ["adr-2605231400-kotoba-datomic-holochain-iso-substrate" "adr-2605192100-etzhayyim-mission-charter" "adr-2605192415-etzhayyim-religious-corp-daemon-architecture" "adr-2605214000-etzhayyim-murakumo-mesh-no-vke-and-lexicon-port-rules" "adr-2606012100-donation-funded-operation-and-compute-node-donation" "adr-2606014500-one-worker-many-wasm-actors" "adr-2606014600-wasm-actor-runtime-gateway-loader-componentize-py" "adr-2605250715-tatekata-construction-actor" "adr-2605261100-hikari-energy-actor"], :adr/authoritative_for ["kotoba-os — content-addressed WASM-first unikernel runtime shape for kotoba" "WASM Component Model as the kotoba-os userland execution unit (control program / agent / cell)" "OT/PLC scan-cycle-as-Datom-transaction control model (soft-RT R0; hard-RT/SIL gated)" "kotoba-os as a k8s-schedulable microVM/runwasi node class (Murakumo kubelet)" "machine management-plane state modelled as Datoms (Datomic-for-the-OS)"]}]
